NAME: flint-lite-operator
LAST DEPLOYED: Fri Oct  2 22:21:22 2026
NAMESPACE: flint-system
STATUS: deployed
REVISION: 1
TEST SUITE: None
NOTES:
flint-lite-operator 1.56.0 is installed in flint-system.

Default hub image: flint-pnfs:step5-dev
Watching: all namespaces

Create a share:

  kubectl apply -f - <<'EOF'
  apiVersion: chert.us/v1alpha1
  kind: FlintShare
  metadata:
    name: tenant-a
    namespace: flint-system
  spec:
    persistence:
      size: 20Gi
    # Tier to a bucket (optional — without it the PVC is the only copy
    # of the data). The bucket must already exist, with VERSIONING ON.
    # bucket: my-team-flint
    # keyPrefix: tenant-a/          # must end with "/"; immutable
    # credentialsSecretRef: flint-s3   # "" = IRSA / instance role
  EOF

Watch it come up (a tiered share is legitimately "Starting" for
minutes — it claims the volume epoch, which may wait out a dead
holder's lease, and may import the whole bucket, all before its
listener binds):

  kubectl get flintshares -A -w
  kubectl describe flintshare tenant-a -n flint-system

Mount it from any pod in the cluster — the export is the SERVER ROOT,
so the path is "/", not the hub's on-disk directory:

  status.address is host:port, and mount will not take it whole
  (host:2049:/ is refused). Split it — this also survives an
  advertiseAddress on a port other than 2049:

    ADDR=$(kubectl get fsh tenant-a -n flint-system -o jsonpath='{.status.address}')
    mount -t nfs4 -o vers=4.1,nconnect=4,port=${ADDR##*:} ${ADDR%:*}:/ /mnt/project

Two things worth knowing:

  * Deleting a FlintShare keeps its PVC (reclaim: Retain by default)
    and NEVER touches the bucket.
  * At most one share may own a bucket subtree. A second one is
    refused with a Conflict condition naming the winner — two hubs on
    one prefix means that when one dies, the other takes it over.
